; ======================================================================
;  pi3stubs.pi3 - WHAT THIS BOARD DOES NOT DO, SAID OUT LOUD.
;
;  Every capability this board declares 0 still needs its Hw* seam to
;  EXIST, and this is where they are. Its counterpart is
;  ArduinoQ/Board/qstubs_q.unoq, which does the same job on that board
;  for a longer list.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY A BOARD SUPPLIES A SEAM IT CANNOT ANSWER
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  An ABSENT procedure is a link error naming a symbol, which tells a
;  reader nothing about the board. A seam that is optional in places
;  produces a board that builds and then behaves differently for a reason
;  nobody can see. So a board supplies the WHOLE seam, the gate in front
;  of it refuses the command, and the procedure behind the gate answers
;  the way a board with no such hardware should - which is what the
;  sections below are.
;
;  NOTHING HERE IS REACHED IN NORMAL USE. RequireCap() in
;  Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi reads the capability flag and refuses the command
;  with a whole sentence before any of these is called. They exist so
;  that the image LINKS and so that a flag raised before its backend is
;  written fails in a way somebody can read.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EVERY ZERO HERE SAYS WHICH KIND OF ZERO IT IS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi is explicit that 0 covers both "the board has no
;  such hardware" and "no backend has been written for it yet", because
;  from the operator's seat those are the same fact and the same honest
;  refusal. They are NOT the same fact for the next person to work here,
;  so each section below says which it is - and on this board most of
;  them are the second, sometimes with a working driver sitting in the
;  tree that this image deliberately does not carry.
;
;  THIS IS NOT FEATURE-GATING TO SAVE SPACE. hal.pbi forbids that in as
;  many words and nothing here is hidden that works.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi (every #HW_* code these
;  answer with), after RaspberryPi3/Lib/pl011_console.pi3 (PrintN) and
;  after the A/B module (pi3_dtb). House rule 10: this file includes
;  nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ======================================================================
;  #CAP_CONSOLE = 0 - THERE IS A FRAMEBUFFER AND THERE IS NO SURFACE.
;
;  This is the one group on this board where 0 is the most misleading
;  answer available, so it is worth being exact about what is and is not
;  there. RaspberryPi3/Lib/framebuffer.pbi allocates an HDMI framebuffer
;  through the firmware mailbox and PRESENTS IT - that was proven on this
;  silicon in build 6, and the panel showed text.
;
;  What does not exist is the HwCon* surface: the console grid, the
;  damage tracking, the text renderer and the tiering that let the shared
;  core and a payload DRAW on it. That is milestone 3, and until it is
;  written a board that claimed #CAP_CONSOLE = 1 would offer a payload a
;  drawing surface that answers every call with a zero.
;
;  #HW_TIER_CPU IS THE ONLY HONEST ANSWER TO A QUESTION THAT SHOULD NOT
;  BE ASKED. The seam has three tiers and no 'none', because a board
;  that answers this is a board with a surface; here the gate in front
;  of every caller is #CAP_CONSOLE = 0, so nothing reaches it. It says
;  CPU rather than an invented fourth value so that a reader is not sent
;  looking for a constant that does not exist.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i HwConBacklight(pct.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwConCapture()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwConFrameBegin()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
