; ======================================================================
;  #CAP_GPIO = 0 - NO BACKEND, AND THE HARDWARE IS RIGHT THERE.
;
;  The BCM2837's GPIO block is at $3F200000 and this monitor already
;  writes it: RaspberryPi3/Lib/uart.pbi muxes GPIO 14 and 15 to the
;  PL011 and runs the old-style GPPUD/GPPUDCLK pull sequence, and
;  RaspberryPi3/Lib/sdhost.pbi muxes 48 to 53 for the card. So this 0 is
;  "no backend has been written", not "Anvil cannot reach the pins".
;
;  HwGpioCount() ANSWERS 0 AND NOT 54. A count with no backend behind it
;  would make `gpio` print a table of pins it cannot read, which is worse
;  than refusing: every row would be a plausible-looking lie.
;
;  WHAT WRITING THIS BACKEND ACTUALLY NEEDS, so nobody starts it thinking
;  it is an afternoon: the reserved-pin list. This board has the console
;  on 14 and 15, the card on 48 to 53, and the radio on 34 to 39 in ALT3 -
;  and driving any of those from `gpio` takes the machine down under
;  itself. HwGpioReservedReason() is the seam for saying so per pin, and
;  it is the part that has to be right before the write half is offered.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i HwGpioAltGet(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwGpioCount()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
Procedure.i HwGpioLevelGet(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure
