; ======================================================================
;  #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0 - AND ON THIS BOARD THAT IS NOT A PLATFORM LIMIT.
;
;  The Arduino UNO Q answers 0 here because UEFI runs it at EL1 and there
;  is no EL2 from which to install one. This board is different: the
;  firmware normally hands a Pi 3 over at EL2, which is exactly where a
;  kernel handoff has to be done from, so the capability is reachable and
;  it is simply not written.
;
;  IT IS NOT WRITTEN BECAUSE THE PIECES AROUND IT ARE NOT. Dropping an
;  arm64 Image to EL1 means handing it a device tree - this board has
;  one, pinned at $1000000 - and it means the caches and MMU this monitor
;  just turned on being put back exactly as the kernel expects to find
;  them, which on this part has never been done and cannot be proven
;  without a kernel to boot. That is a milestone of its own.
; ======================================================================

Procedure HwBootToEl1(entry.i, dtb.i)
  ; Unreachable: #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0 gates every caller. If it is ever
  ; reached, a capability was declared without a backend, and saying so
  ; is far better than branching somewhere with the wrong exception
  ; level and the caches in an unknown state.
  PrintN("!! HwBootToEl1 was reached on a board that declares #CAP_BOOT_EL1 = 0.")
  PrintN("   That is a bug in this board's capability declaration, not in the")
  PrintN("   command. Nothing was done and nothing was branched to.")
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  HwFirmwareDtb() - AND THIS ONE IS A REAL ANSWER, NOT A STUB.
;
;  It sits in this file because it is one procedure and has no family to
;  live with, not because this board cannot answer it. The Pi 3 CAN: the
;  device tree is at $1000000, pinned there by the boot arrangement -
;  Pi3UpdateConfigure() refuses any other value, the loader passes it in
;  x0 when it branches, and Pi3BootMemory() has already walked its header
;  and checked its magic and its length before this monitor printed
;  anything.
;
;  SO IT RETURNS pi3_dtb AND NOT A CONSTANT. If boot validation refused,
;  pi3_dtb is left at whatever it was and the boot stopped long before
;  here; if it succeeded, this is a pointer somebody has already checked.
;  Returning $1000000 unconditionally would be the same number with none
;  of that behind it.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i HwFirmwareDtb()
  ProcedureReturn pi3_dtb
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CONSTANTS AND GLOBALS THE SHARED CORE READS FROM ITS BOARD.
;
;  Not a seam and not a capability: a handful of names that shared files
;  reference directly and that only a board can fill in. The Arduino
;  UNO Q keeps its set at the foot of qstubs_q.unoq for the same reason.
; ======================================================================

; The console's own base, which the banner prints so an operator can see
; WHICH port the text they are reading came out of. A real address here,
; unlike the UNO Q, whose console is a firmware protocol with no base.
#UART_BASE = $3F201000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE BOOT COUNTER - THIS BOARD HAS NONE, AND SAYS SO.
;
;  Anvil/Core/uptime.pbi reads a cell that survives a reset and holds a
;  magic and a count, so `uptime` can say how many times the board has
;  restarted. On the Pi 4 a library maintains it; nothing maintains one
;  here, and writing one would be new work in a lane that has been told
;  not to add libraries that do not exist yet.
;
;  SO THE MAGIC IS ZERO AND CAN NEVER MATCH, which is what makes uptime
;  report the count as not available instead of reporting a number that
;  came from whatever was in memory. The address still has to be one that
;  is safe to READ, because the read happens before the magic is tested:
;  the loader's handoff page is mapped, owned by the boot arrangement,
;  and never holds this magic.
;
;  WHAT A REAL ONE WOULD NEED, so nobody starts it by accident: a cell
;  outside both the monitor's BSS and the loader's, in a region
;  memmap.pi3 declares so that `fill` cannot reach it, incremented once
;  per boot before anything can fault, and reset by a confirmed update.
;  That is a milestone, not a constant.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SAFETY_COUNT_ADDR = $1FF0000      ; the loader handoff page - readable,
                                   ; and never this magic
#SAFETY_MAGIC      = $00000000     ; can never match; the count is absent

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Globals the shared line editor and commands read. On the Pi 4 they
;  live in that board's display globals; this board has no display
;  service and no USB input, so they are constants that say so.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global gMouseTried.i = 1     ; 1 = "already tried", so ReadLine never asks
Global gKbdH.i = 0           ; no USB HID keyboard on this board
Global gScreen.i = 0         ; no console surface yet - see #CAP_CONSOLE
Global gLastCR.i = 0         ; the line editor's CR/LF de-duplication state
Global gBootTicks.i = 0      ; the counter at entry, set by Main()
Global gBase.i = 0           ; the `base` command's address base
Global gMbx.i = 0            ; the mailbox library keeps its own state

; ======================================================================
;  THE SERVICE HOOKS THE PROMPT CALLS ON EVERY SPIN.
;
;  Anvil's core is one set of bytes for every board, so the command loop
;  and the line editor call these unconditionally. A board that has none
;  of the hardware behind them still has to make the NAMES resolve, and
;  the honest answer is a no-op that says which kind of nothing it is.
;
;  THE TWO THAT RETURN VALUES ARE THE DANGEROUS ONES. KeyboardChar() and
;  NetConsoleGetc() feed the line editor; the only safe answers here are
;  "no character" and "idle", and anything else is a phantom keystroke in
;  somebody's command.
; ======================================================================

; No pixel surface yet - see #CAP_CONSOLE in the board file.
Procedure ScreenPump()
EndProcedure

Procedure ScreenBannerTick()
EndProcedure

Procedure ScreenServiceTick()
EndProcedure

; No USB host in this image, so no keyboard and no mouse to attach.
Procedure MouseTryAttach()
EndProcedure

Procedure MouseTick()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i KeyboardChar()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 = no character
EndProcedure

; No touch controller on this board at all.
Procedure TouchTick()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE NETWORK SERVICE HOOKS. #CAP_NET is 0 here, so there is no link, no
;  lease, no console peer and no connection - but the prompt calls all
;  six on every spin because on a board that HAS them, a lease renewing
;  or a handshake finishing while nobody is typing is exactly what they
;  are for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure NetConsolePump()
EndProcedure

; A local keystroke cannot contend with a remote console session here:
; there is no remote owner to release.
Procedure NetConsoleClaimLocal()
EndProcedure

Procedure NetConsoleRearm()
EndProcedure

; The link-local address defence (RFC 3927 section 2.5): a second
; conflicting ARP inside ten seconds must end in the address being
; dropped rather than defended again. This board can never have taken
; one.
Procedure NetConsoleLlTick()
EndProcedure

Procedure NetDhcpTick()
EndProcedure

Procedure TcpTick()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i NetConsoleGetc()
  ProcedureReturn -1           ; below zero = idle
EndProcedure

Procedure WifiLinkTick()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  CACHE MAINTENANCE - and on this board it is REAL, not a stub.
;
;  The memory family flushes a range before running code that was just
;  written into it, so the instruction fetch sees the new bytes rather
;  than whatever the instruction cache had. On a board with the caches
;  off that can be nothing; this monitor turns them ON during boot, so it
;  has to be the real maintenance or `run` would branch into stale lines.
;
;  CLEAN TO THE POINT OF UNIFICATION, THEN INVALIDATE THE INSTRUCTION
;  LINE, which is the ARM-documented pair for exactly this: `dc cvau`
;  pushes the data the processor wrote out to where an instruction fetch
;  will see it, and `ic ivau` throws away any instruction line that was
;  already holding the old bytes. One without the other is the failure
;  that produces no message at all.
;
;  SIXTY-FOUR BYTES IS THE A53's CACHE LINE, the same as the A72's, so
;  the other board's step carries over unchanged.
;
;  THE ADDRESS GOES THROUGH A GLOBAL because an ASM block cannot take a
;  local as an operand here. Its own procedure, so the loop around it
;  stays readable.
; ======================================================================
Global gPi3FlushAddr.i

Procedure Pi3CacheLine()
  ASM
    adrp x9, global_gpi3flushaddr
    add  x9, x9, #:lo12:global_gpi3flushaddr
    ldr  x9, [x9]
    dc cvau, x9
    ic ivau, x9
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure CacheFlushRange(lo.i, hi.i)
  Define a.i
  If hi < lo
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  a = (lo >> 6) << 6
  While a <= hi
    gPi3FlushAddr = a
    Pi3CacheLine()
    a = a + 64
  Wend
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  AUTOBOOT - THIS BOARD HAS SOMETHING BETTER AND DOES NOT NEED IT.
;
;  On the Pi 4 an autoboot cell in preserved memory holds an entry point
;  to run without being asked. This board's equivalent is the A/B slot
;  machinery itself: the loader already chooses what runs, verifies it,
;  gives it a trial and falls back if it does not confirm. An autoboot
;  cell beside that would be a second, weaker answer to a question this
;  board has already answered well.
;
;  So there is no armed entry and nothing to store, and Banner() reports
;  none - which is true rather than merely unimplemented.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i AutoEntry()
  ProcedureReturn 0            ; 0 = no autoboot entry armed
EndProcedure

Procedure AutoSet(a.i)
  ; Nothing to store. The command that calls this says so rather than
  ; falling silent.
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  RunAt(a) - BRANCH INTO A PAYLOAD AND COME BACK.
;
;  This is `run` and `go`, and it is the one place this monitor hands the
;  processor to code it did not compile. It is here rather than in a stub
;  section because it is REAL: it is short because there is very little a
;  board has to do, not because there is nothing.
;
;  THE ADDRESS IS NOT CHECKED HERE and that is not an omission. The
;  command that got it has already been through the address guard and the
;  monitor-window refusals, which is where an address is judged; a second
;  opinion at the branch would either agree, and be noise, or disagree,
;  and leave two answers to one question.
;
;  ZERO IS THE ONE VALUE REFUSED, because it is what every "no entry
;  point" answer in this tree is spelled as - AutoEntry() above returns it
;  - and branching to it would turn a missing payload into a fault at
;  address nothing.
;
;  THE INSTRUCTION CACHE IS THE CALLER'S PROBLEM AND IT IS HANDLED. A
;  payload that arrived over the serial line was WRITTEN as data with the
;  caches on, so the instruction fetch at its entry point could see
;  whatever was there before. Anvil/Core/memcmd.pbi calls
;  CacheFlushRange() over the received range before it calls this, which
;  is exactly why the real maintenance above is not a no-op on this
;  board.
;
;  THE PAYLOAD IS ENTERED WITH THIS MONITOR RESIDENT. It returns here,
;  and everything this board owns - the card, the console, the page
;  tables - is still owned when it does.
; ======================================================================
Procedure RunAt(a.i)
  If a = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  Pi3EnterPayload(a)
EndProcedure
